+    rules:  # v1.4, knowledge/codebook-v1.4-proposal.md
+      - "A harm type is coded when the work demonstrates, measures or argues the mechanism for its own object, and every assigned value has its own passage. A harm named only in background, in a definitions table or in a related-work passage is left uncoded. A review or taxonomy codes the harm types that structure its own synthesis as a section, table row or coded category."
+      - "Allocational harms are separated from representational harms first (Gallegos et al. 2024, Table 1). Direct_Discrimination and Indirect_Discrimination require an allocation or a decision about persons or groups, such as hiring, benefits, screening-in or a risk score. Output that depicts or describes a group is a representational harm."
+      - "Direct_Discrimination applies when the decision changes with the group attribute itself, stated in the input or the rule. Indirect_Discrimination applies when an apparently neutral criterion or proxy such as a name, postcode, prior service contact or digital access produces the disadvantage. When the work classifies the discrimination itself, its classification is coded."
+      - "Disparate_Performance covers differences in system quality between groups or language varieties, such as accuracy, error rate or recognition. A difference in decision outcomes is discrimination, a difference in quality is Disparate_Performance, and a work that shows both codes both."
+      - "Exclusionary_Norms applies when the norm of a dominant group acts as default and other groups are left out or devalued, such as a male default, cis-centric answers or the assumption of digitally skilled clients. Stereotyping ascribes attributes to a group, Erasure leaves a group or its experience out, Misrepresentation generalises an unrepresentative depiction to a group."
